    <string name="guideline_accessibility_contrast_excerpt">Provide enough contrast between text and its background to keep content legible. A minimum ratio of 4.5:1 is recommended for normal text (WCAG AA).</string>
    <string name="guideline_accessibility_contrast_section">Accessibility — Color and Contrast</string>
    <string name="guideline_color_excerpt">Use color to enhance your interface and communicate status. Always pair color with another indicator so people who are color-blind get the same information.</string>
    <string name="guideline_color_section">Color</string>
    <string name="guideline_dark_mode_excerpt">Prefer dynamic semantic colors that automatically adapt to Dark Mode. Avoid hardcoded color values that look good in one appearance but fail in another.</string>
    <string name="guideline_dark_mode_section">Dark Mode</string>
    <string name="guideline_inclusive_color_excerpt">Don\'t rely solely on color to communicate important information. Always pair color with a text label or symbol so that people who are color-blind can understand your UI.</string>
    <string name="guideline_inclusive_color_section">Color — Inclusive color</string>
    <string name="guideline_interactive_color_excerpt">Choose a single consistent color for interactive elements to establish clear affordance. People associate that color with tappable, so inconsistent use creates confusion.</string>
    <string name="guideline_interactive_color_section">Color — Interactive elements</string>
    <string name="guideline_materials_excerpt">Use materials and semantic colors to communicate hierarchy, depth, and vibrancy. Semantic surfaces automatically provide the right appearance in both Light and Dark Mode.</string>
    <string name="guideline_materials_section">Materials</string>
